Q: Get unknown word that follows a specific word A: <p>Finding the word that immediately follows &quot;post&quot;-comma-space, is a task best left to a regular expression.</p>
<p>Use <code>\b</code> before <code>post</code> (and the comma after) in the pattern to ensure that only the whole word &quot;post&quot; is matched.</p>
<p>Use <code>\K</code> to restart the full string match -- avoiding a lookbehind will improve pattern efficiency.</p>
<p>Codes: (<a href="https://3v4l.org/BT8KT" rel="nofollow noreferrer">Demo</a>)</p>
<pre><code>if (preg_match('/\bpost, \K\S+/', $string, $match)) {
    echo $match[0];
}
</code></pre>
<p>Or</p>
<pre><code>$string = preg_replace('/.*\bpost, (\S+).*/', '$1', $string, 1, $count);
if ($count) {
    echo $string;
}
</code></pre>
<p>For related scenarios where multiple matches are desired, <a href="https://stackoverflow.com/a/79925342/2943403"><code>preg_match_all()</code> and <code>preg_split()</code> are viable tools</a>.</p>

Q: Pygments syntax highlighter in Python Tkinter text widget A: <p>To highlight certain words, you can do this:</p>
<pre><code>textarea.tag_remove(&quot;tagname&quot;, &quot;1.0&quot;, tkinter.END)
first = &quot;1.0&quot;
while(True):
    first = textarea.search(&quot;word_you_are_looking_for&quot;, first, nocase=False, stopindex=tkinter.END)
    if not first:
        break
    last = first + &quot;+&quot; + str(len(&quot;word_you_are_looking_for&quot;)) + &quot;c&quot;
    textarea.tag_add(&quot;tagname&quot;, first, last)
    first = last
textarea.tag_config(&quot;tagname&quot;, foreground=&quot;#00FF00&quot;)
</code></pre>

